{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target identifies anthropogenic heat as the 'dominant driver' and singles out population density and per-capita energy consumption as the cross-city modulator. However, the strongest known single mechanism explaining why some cities have larger nocturnal UHI than others is urban geometry—specifically reduced sky view factor in dense street canyons, which suppresses radiative cooling. The target buries geometry as a mere countercondition rather than treating it as the dominant explanation, and its own counterconditions concede the gap may scale with sky view factor rather than per-capita energy. As stated, it misattributes the dominant cross-city modulator.","premise":"On a clear, calm night the urban–rural temperature gap is governed primarily by differential radiative cooling: rural surfaces with high sky view factor radiate freely to the cold sky, while urban street canyons with low sky view factor lose longwave radiation inefficiently. Anthropogenic heat is a secondary, variable input.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"cf6e70b1398c","quote":"If the gap scales with sky view factor rather than per-capita energy consumption, geometry dominates over waste heat"}]}]}